causeTitle.format:
LEGAL NOTICE
(Cease & Desist — Under the Copyright Act, 1957)

To,
{respondent}
{respondentAddress}

From,
{applicant}
Through: {advocateName}, Advocate
{advocateAddress}

prayerTemplate:
You are hereby called upon to, within {response_days} ({response_days_words}) days of receipt of this notice:
(a) IMMEDIATELY CEASE AND DESIST from all acts of infringement, including reproduction, communication to the public, public performance, broadcasting, adaptation, translation, distribution, sale, offering for sale, importation, and any other exclusive act reserved to my client under Section 14 of the Copyright Act, 1957, in relation to the work titled '{work_title}';
(b) TAKE DOWN and permanently remove the infringing material located at {urls_or_locations} and confirm removal in writing with screenshots;
(c) DELIVER UP to my client all infringing copies, masters, plates, films, files and means of reproduction for destruction in terms of Section 58 of the Copyright Act, 1957;
(d) Render a true and faithful account of all profits earned through the said infringement and pay such account, OR alternatively pay damages in the sum of Rs. {damages_amount}/- at my client's election;
(e) Furnish a written UNDERTAKING, on affidavit, that you shall not, directly or indirectly, by yourself or through any agent or assign, infringe my client's copyright in the said work or any other work of my client in future;
(f) Reimburse the cost of this notice and legal expenses.

Failing the above, my client shall be constrained to file a suit for permanent injunction, delivery up, damages, and account of profits before the competent court of jurisdiction under Section 62 of the Copyright Act, 1957 — which permits the suit to be instituted at the place where my client resides or carries on business — and to seek interim and ad-interim injunctions including ex-parte and Anton Piller / John Doe orders, entirely at your risk, cost, and consequences.

My client further reserves the right to initiate criminal proceedings against you under Section 63 of the Copyright Act, 1957, which provides for imprisonment of a term not less than 6 (six) months but extending to 3 (three) years and fine, and Section 63B (computer programme infringement). My client also reserves the right to seek John Doe / Ashok Kumar orders against unknown infringers.
